\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}
\title{Nineteen hexagons through every edge: the remaining finite lower bound}
\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{theorem}\label{main}
No finite simple quartic graph of girth six on at most thirty-five
vertices has exactly nineteen hexagons through every edge. Thus
\(n(4,6,19)\ge36\).
\end{theorem}
This recovers the remaining clause of the row $\lambda=19$--$20$ in
Table~2 of Goedgebeur--Jooken, \emph{Exhaustive generation of
edge-girth-regular graphs}, arXiv:2401.08271v2, physical page~18.
The separate twenty-hexagon clause is already acquired. Neither clause
asserts existence at order thirty-six or nonexistence at every order.

\section{The local equations}
Fix an edge $uv$ in a hypothetical graph. Its two trees of depth two,
with $uv$ omitted at the first step, contain twenty-six distinct vertices.
An identification creates a cycle of length at most five. The only
possible additional edges among these vertices join opposite leaves;
every other additional edge also creates a forbidden short cycle.
Write the nine leaves on each side as three branches of three leaves.
Edges between a pair of opposite branches form a matching, because two
neighbours in a single branch create a quadrangle. Each such cross edge
gives one hexagon through $uv$, and every hexagon through $uv$ occurs in
this way. Consequently there are precisely nineteen cross edges.
Writing their nine matching sizes as $3-d_{ij}$ gives
\[
  0\le d_{ij}\le3,\qquad \sum_{i,j=0}^2d_{ij}=8.                 \tag{1}
\]
There are eight unfinished leaf incidences on each side. They must meet
vertices outside the twenty-six vertices: an extra opposite-leaf edge
would give a twentieth hexagon through $uv$.

We use the acquired Moore-tree, root-pair and saturation arguments from
the twenty-hexagon component, with the following explicit changes.
Its nonexistence theorem is not used as a premise.

\begin{lemma}\label{capacity}
For a positive unfinished-degree profile $P$, put
\(f(P)=(24-\sum_{s\in P}s^2)/2\). If $P_L,P_R$ are the two profiles,
then $f(P_L)\le b(P_R)$ and $f(P_R)\le b(P_L)$, where
\[
\begin{array}{c|rrrrrrrrrr}
P&1^8&2,1^6&3,1^5&2^2,1^4&3,2,1^3&2^3,1^2&3^2,1^2&3,2^2,1&2^4&3^2,2\\\hline
f(P)&8&7&5&6&4&5&2&3&4&1\\
b(P)&7&7&7&7&6&7&4&5&5&3
\end{array}                                                     \tag{2}
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
There are thirty-eight hexagons through $u$, since every incident edge
is in nineteen and each hexagon uses two incident edges. Nineteen
contain $uv$. A right leaf with unfinished degree $s$ supplies
$\binom{3-s}{2}$ of the others, by choosing two of its cross neighbours
in distinct left branches. Thus the number still to be supplied by
outside common neighbours of left leaves is
\[
 19-\sum_{i=1}^9\binom{3-s_i}{2}
       =\frac{24-\sum_i s_i^2}{2}=f(P_R).                       \tag{3}
\]
An outside neighbour meets at most one leaf per branch and hence at
most three on one side. A pair of leaves cannot have two such common
neighbours. To obtain an upper bound depending only on $P_L$, partition
its eight distinguished incidences into parts of size at most three,
with no repeated leaf in a part and no repeated pair of leaves among
parts. The maximum of $\sum_B\binom{|B|}{2}$ is the corresponding
$b(P_L)$ in (2). The complete first-appearance recursion inserts each
incidence in every admissible existing part or one new part. A separate
checker evaluates every one of the $4\,140$ partitions of eight
distinguished positions for each of the ten profiles. Both give the
listed maxima. Ignoring the branch restriction only enlarges them.
The analogous argument at $v$ proves the second inequality.
\end{proof}

Let $L_0,L_1,L_2$ be the left branches, and let $a_i$ count their cross
edges. Once the left leaves are saturated, the necessary root-pair
equations are, for distinct $i,j,k$,
\[
 \sum_w |N(w)\cap L_i|\,|N(w)\cap L_j|=a_k.                    \tag{4}
\]
Indeed, if $b_{ij}$ counts hexagons through the two root edges in
branches $i,j$, then $a_i+b_{ij}+b_{ik}=19$ and
$a_0+a_1+a_2=19$. Solving gives $b_{ij}=a_k$. Common neighbours of
the indicated leaves give exactly these hexagons; girth six ensures
that their paths are simple. Saturation fixes all terms in (4).
There are identical equations on the right.

\section{Forest normalization and literal coverage}
There are $8\,451$ matrices in (1), in $154$ orbits under independent
row and column permutations and transpose. The preparation script
and the C++ search independently construct the entire integer set
and verify this orbit cover. These operations are actual
automorphisms of the two rooted trees.

The acquired partial-matching forest theorem applies to the six sets
of three leaves. Choose a forest of positive matching blocks and root
each component. For an edge directed from parent to child with matching
size $k$, retain every $k$-subset of the parent labels, in increasing
order, matched to the first $k$ child labels in that order. A fresh
child permutation achieves this form; applying it to \emph{all}
incident blocks leaves already normalized forest blocks unchanged.
Proceeding away from the roots proves coverage. Blocks outside the
forest retain every partial injective matching. Reverse orientations
use the transposed prescription. This is a list meeting every orbit,
not an assertion of one representative per orbit.

For a forest block there are $\binom3k$ choices. For a free block of
deficit $0,1,2,3$ there are respectively $6,18,9,1$ choices. The
verified forests give $24\,684\,399$ choices in the complete product.
Restricting normalization to full blocks would give
$13\,092\,060\,159$ choices. These are exact matching inventories;
neither is a graph-class count or a measured runtime claim.

The graph search visits every child of every surviving prefix. It
rejects a prefix only when an added cross edge closes a quadrangle.
The graph is bipartite, so this is precisely the short-cycle test
needed at this stage. At a complete matching assignment it tests (2).
Every remaining graph is retained or matched to an earlier retained
graph by an explicitly checked bijection. Nauty proposes the lookup
key and permutation only. The program verifies bijectivity, preservation
of the unordered root edge, and equality of every transported adjacency
row. A false merge therefore aborts. A false split merely retains an
additional graph, which must also be processed. No completeness of a
precomputed catalogue is a premise.

The closed execution visits $6\,531\,610$ prefixes and
$2\,068\,524$ girth-admissible matching assignments. It checks
$1\,721\,868$ literal maps after (2), retaining $144\,423$ graphs.
The same changed normalization was first run for the acquired
twenty-hexagon case. Its complete retained list and the acquired list
of $31\,501$ graphs cover one another by checked literal maps.
That comparison validates the implementation; the forest theorem
and exhaustive traversal prove coverage for the present parameter.

\section{Two outside boundaries}
For each retained graph, partition the eight left incidences by their
actual outside neighbour. The first-appearance recursion includes
every partition. A part may contain no repeated leaf and at most
one leaf from each branch. A pair of leaves may occur together in
at most one part and may not already have an old common neighbour.
These are necessary quadrangle restrictions. While forming parts,
the new common-neighbour counts may not exceed (4); at the end they
must equal (4). The right side is treated independently. Right
attachments do not alter the left leaf neighbourhoods.

There are $207\,349$ surviving side partitions, and $1\,280$ root
graphs have a surviving partition on both sides. For each pair of
partitions, enumerate every partial one-to-one matching between
their parts, with combined size at most four. Such a match means
that the two parts name the same outside vertex. Distinct parts
on one side cannot be merged, and every actual outside vertex
is either one part or a matched pair. This proves complete coverage.
Cases of order greater than thirty-five are discarded. Every other
case is checked for girth, and every surviving reuse is checked by
a literal adjacency-preserving bijection on the entire partial graph.
All twenty-six original vertices are now saturated, so preserving
the original root under these later maps is unnecessary.

The join stage has $28\,611\,096$ choices. Of these, $28\,800$
exceed the order limit and $28\,061\,948$ have a short cycle.
The remaining $520\,348$ checked maps cover $8\,446$ retained
partial graphs. Of these, $4\,676$ already have more than nineteen
hexagons on some edge, a permanent obstruction.

For each other graph, first enumerate all subsets of possible edges
between its deficient vertices. Degree overflow and distance less
than five before insertion are forbidden. Every old-to-old edge in
an actual completion appears in one of these subsets. Next list
all remaining old incidences and partition their endpoints into new
vertices by first appearance. No old vertex may be repeated at a
new neighbour; its degree is at most four, and short cycles are
forbidden. If the current graph has $n$ vertices, at most $35-n$
new vertices may occur, and more than $4(35-n)$ remaining incidences
are impossible. The empty incidence list is included.

All old vertices are now saturated. Edges among new vertices or
farther outside are not enumerated. To justify this, if every vertex
at distance at most two from $x$ is saturated in a partial quartic
graph, every hexagon through $x$ in a completion is already present.
Every edge of such a hexagon has an endpoint within two steps of
$x$ along the hexagon; no new edge can leave such an endpoint.
This is the acquired saturation lemma, with its hypothesis checked
on the actual partial graph.

There are $806\,992$ old-edge choices. The incidence capacity test
rejects $767\,049$. Across the others there are $4\,626$
girth-admissible incidence extensions within the order cap. For
every one the verifier finds an edge $xy$ with saturated radius
two at $x$ and counts a number of hexagons different from nineteen.
It counts all simple alternate paths of length five; stopping after
the count exceeds nineteen is sufficient for a rejection. If any
extension lacked such a protected mismatch, the verification would
fail rather than certify an exclusion.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{main}]
A hypothetical graph supplies the two Moore trees, a normalized
cross-matching assignment, two outside partitions, their joining,
and a second-boundary extension within the order cap. Every reuse
is an actual checked isomorphism. Every complete branch is rejected
by a necessary obstruction or a hexagon count protected against
all undecided farther edges. Hence no hypothetical graph exists.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Attribution and verification.}
The lower bound is an original source result, not a newly resolved
conjecture. The preceding component supplies the Moore-tree,
root-pair and boundary architecture and the literal-map checks.
The partial-matching forest theorem, its present implementation,
the eight-incidence capacities and the new complete executions
are the additional work here. Root supplied the earlier local
geometry and requested this remaining clause; the twenty-hexagon
certificate author supplied the reused checker architecture.
The present author previously reviewed those components, which
does not make this new proof independently reviewed. Its distinct
review is a separate record. No priority, whole-source recovery,
new graph count, Lean or compression-measurement claim is made.
